1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:04,513
Everything we've built works when we
remember to run it, in the right order,

2
00:00:04,513 --> 00:00:05,650
with the right flags.

3
00:00:06,150 --> 00:00:10,425
That isn't a routine, and it won't survive
a busy week or a new teammate.

4
00:00:10,925 --> 00:00:15,216
Meanwhile the skill will be edited again,
and the models under it will change

5
00:00:15,216 --> 00:00:16,275
without telling us.

6
00:00:16,775 --> 00:00:21,120
Here, you'll make the right check the easy
one: one short command after every edit, a

7
00:00:21,120 --> 00:00:24,750
longer one before sharing, and one page
that says what the team agreed.

8
00:00:25,967 --> 00:00:28,642
Let's start with what each check costs.

9
00:00:29,142 --> 00:00:33,717
We now have five ways to check the skill,
and they differ enormously in cost.

10
00:00:34,217 --> 00:00:39,142
Lint, the oracle and a re-grade start no
agent, so for code graders they're free.

11
00:00:39,642 --> 00:00:44,062
The other two are new here: a quick run of
the stimuli that code can check, and a

12
00:00:44,062 --> 00:00:45,317
full run of everything.

13
00:00:45,817 --> 00:00:50,253
On two models, the quick one spends ten
agent runs, and the full one spends

14
00:00:50,253 --> 00:00:50,667
thirty.

15
00:00:51,867 --> 00:00:56,567
The rule is to use the cheapest check that
can answer the question we actually have.

16
00:00:57,067 --> 00:01:00,017
After editing an eval file, that's lint.

17
00:01:00,517 --> 00:01:04,848
After changing a grader, it's the oracle,
which tries a correct answer and an

18
00:01:04,848 --> 00:01:05,692
untouched file.

19
00:01:06,192 --> 00:01:10,542
After changing a pattern, a budget or a
rubric, a re-grade says what the new

20
00:01:10,542 --> 00:01:12,717
version makes of trials already saved.

21
00:01:13,217 --> 00:01:17,742
A script grader can't be re-graded,
because the workspace it reads is gone.

22
00:01:18,242 --> 00:01:21,467
So a change to a script goes back to the
oracle.

23
00:01:21,967 --> 00:01:26,465
After every edit to the skill, the quick
run asks whether anything code can check

24
00:01:26,465 --> 00:01:28,242
has broken, or gone over budget.

25
00:01:28,742 --> 00:01:33,425
Finally, before sharing the skill, or when
a model changes, the full run asks whether

26
00:01:33,425 --> 00:01:34,692
everything still holds.

27
00:01:35,900 --> 00:01:40,600
Three more checks sit outside that order,
because each answers a one-off question.

28
00:01:41,100 --> 00:01:44,655
After a large rewrite, the
with-and-without experiment says whether

29
00:01:44,655 --> 00:01:46,300
the skill still beats no skill.

30
00:01:46,800 --> 00:01:51,284
To learn whether one edit really helped,
we run a before-and-after experiment, then

31
00:01:51,284 --> 00:01:54,525
vally compare, which spends judge calls
and starts no agent.

32
00:01:55,025 --> 00:01:59,862
Finally, cost per passing trial is what to
compare before moving to a different model

33
00:01:59,862 --> 00:02:01,000
or reasoning effort.

34
00:02:02,200 --> 00:02:05,375
For a team, though, the commands are the
easy part.

35
00:02:05,875 --> 00:02:10,427
What drifts is agreement: what deserves a
grader, where grading files are kept, how

36
00:02:10,427 --> 00:02:12,950
a result is reported, and what a run may
cost.

37
00:02:13,450 --> 00:02:16,875
So we'll write those down on one page,
while they're fresh.

38
00:02:17,375 --> 00:02:21,728
People can disagree with something written
down, and that's how the agreement gets

39
00:02:21,728 --> 00:02:22,100
better.

40
00:02:23,300 --> 00:02:27,500
That's the plan; here's what Vally gives
us to build it, starting with tags.

41
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:32,703
When we say tag, we mean a label on a
stimulus: a name and a value, on a line

42
00:02:32,703 --> 00:02:33,375
named tags.

43
00:02:33,875 --> 00:02:38,580
Here the name is tier and the value is
quick; both words are ours, and any words

44
00:02:38,580 --> 00:02:39,050
will do.

45
00:02:39,550 --> 00:02:44,650
All five stimuli in this file get the same
line, because all five are graded by code.

46
00:02:45,867 --> 00:02:48,917
Next, the suite: a named group of stimuli.

47
00:02:49,417 --> 00:02:54,067
It's a list of eval files, optionally
narrowed to the stimuli with a given tag.

48
00:02:54,567 --> 00:02:59,606
Suites are defined in a file named dot
vally dot yaml, in the folder we run Vally

49
00:02:59,606 --> 00:02:59,917
from.

50
00:03:00,417 --> 00:03:04,796
This one defines two; the first is named
quick, which is what we'll type on the

51
00:03:04,796 --> 00:03:05,517
command line.

52
00:03:06,017 --> 00:03:10,363
The quick suite draws on one eval file,
and its filter is matched against each

53
00:03:10,363 --> 00:03:13,317
stimulus's tags, so it keeps the five we
just tagged.

54
00:03:13,817 --> 00:03:18,879
The second, full, lists four eval files
with no filter, so every stimulus in them

55
00:03:18,879 --> 00:03:19,192
runs.

56
00:03:19,692 --> 00:03:24,029
Those four hold the single requests, the
conversation, the loading test, and the

57
00:03:24,029 --> 00:03:24,842
judged wording.

58
00:03:26,067 --> 00:03:30,946
To run a suite, vally eval takes dash dash
suite and the suite's name, in place of

59
00:03:30,946 --> 00:03:32,017
dash e and a file.

60
00:03:32,517 --> 00:03:35,817
Everything else about the command works as
before.

61
00:03:36,317 --> 00:03:41,192
One thing to know: a suite only names
stimuli, and adds no graders of its own.

62
00:03:42,400 --> 00:03:46,752
The five quick stimuli are the ones that
already have budgets, so the quick suite

63
00:03:46,752 --> 00:03:47,450
carries them.

64
00:03:47,950 --> 00:03:52,679
Every trial is checked against a ceiling
on its tokens, on its turns, and on its

65
00:03:52,679 --> 00:03:52,975
time.

66
00:03:55,475 --> 00:03:59,875
The agent is also stopped after a minute,
and whatever it had done by then is

67
00:03:59,875 --> 00:04:00,275
graded.

68
00:04:00,775 --> 00:04:05,282
When a trial goes over a budget, its
stimulus fails, exactly as it would for a

69
00:04:05,282 --> 00:04:05,975
wrong entry.

70
00:04:06,475 --> 00:04:10,500
That's what makes the quick suite a cost
check as well as a quality check.

71
00:04:11,700 --> 00:04:16,625
The last piece is the exit code, which is
what scripts and automated checks act on.

72
00:04:17,125 --> 00:04:22,130
Unless told otherwise, vally eval exits
with zero even when stimuli fail, because

73
00:04:22,130 --> 00:04:23,675
the run itself completed.

74
00:04:24,175 --> 00:04:28,670
Adding dash dash require pass changes
that: any failing stimulus makes the exit

75
00:04:28,670 --> 00:04:31,800
code one, whichever grader failed it,
budgets included.

76
00:04:32,300 --> 00:04:36,325
When a trial ends in error, the exit code
is one either way.

77
00:04:36,825 --> 00:04:39,525
Vally grade accepts the same option.

78
00:04:40,733 --> 00:04:45,024
One kind of failure gets past every
grader: an edit that makes the entries a

79
00:04:45,024 --> 00:04:47,733
little worse, while every pattern still
matches.

80
00:04:48,233 --> 00:04:50,783
That's what the pairwise judge is for.

81
00:04:51,283 --> 00:04:55,858
Vally compare gives it two runs of the
same stimuli, a baseline and a change, and

82
00:04:55,858 --> 00:04:57,608
it scores each pair of answers.

83
00:04:58,108 --> 00:05:02,449
With dash dash fail on regression, the
command exits with one when the change

84
00:05:02,449 --> 00:05:03,633
scored clearly worse.

85
00:05:04,133 --> 00:05:08,383
Clearly worse means that the whole range
around its score is below zero.

86
00:05:08,883 --> 00:05:12,833
Earlier, we tested it by treating the
older skill as the change.

87
00:05:13,333 --> 00:05:17,911
When we ran it, the range reached just
above zero, so the command exited with

88
00:05:17,911 --> 00:05:18,208
zero.

89
00:05:18,708 --> 00:05:23,542
In an earlier run of the same comparison,
the whole range was below zero, and it

90
00:05:23,542 --> 00:05:24,508
exited with one.

91
00:05:25,008 --> 00:05:29,988
It needs a before-and-after experiment and
about eighteen judge calls, so it belongs

92
00:05:29,988 --> 00:05:32,833
before we rely on an edit, not after every
edit.

93
00:05:33,333 --> 00:05:38,333
It also gates on the judged score alone; a
rise in tokens is the budgets' job.

94
00:05:39,533 --> 00:05:42,833
That's how the pieces work; now let's put
them to work.

95
00:05:43,333 --> 00:05:47,842
The tags and the suites file are in place
in our practice folder, exactly as we just

96
00:05:47,842 --> 00:05:48,808
went through them.

97
00:05:49,308 --> 00:05:53,805
Here's a question first: suppose we ran
the quick suite without require pass, and

98
00:05:53,805 --> 00:05:55,858
one trial went over its token budget.

99
00:05:56,358 --> 00:06:00,333
Nothing else fails, and nothing errors:
what would the exit code be?

100
00:06:03,333 --> 00:06:07,411
It would be zero: the budget fails that
stimulus, but without the option, a

101
00:06:07,411 --> 00:06:09,858
failing verdict doesn't change the exit
code.

102
00:06:10,358 --> 00:06:14,283
So anything automated has to add the
option, and we will too.

103
00:06:15,500 --> 00:06:19,050
First, we lint the eval file, since we've
just edited it.

104
00:06:22,300 --> 00:06:26,475
There's one warning, the usual one about
scoring defaults, and no errors.

105
00:06:28,975 --> 00:06:33,330
Now the quick suite, on both models, two
workers at a time, with require pass so

106
00:06:33,330 --> 00:06:34,800
that a failure is an error.

107
00:06:35,300 --> 00:06:39,725
Its results go to a folder of their own,
and it spends ten agent runs.

108
00:06:40,225 --> 00:06:44,825
The table at the end counts the stimuli
that passed, out of five, for each model.

109
00:06:47,133 --> 00:06:51,983
About a minute later, every stimulus has
passed on both models, budgets included.

110
00:06:55,483 --> 00:06:57,683
So what was its exit code?

111
00:06:58,800 --> 00:07:00,800
Zero: nothing failed.

112
00:07:04,000 --> 00:07:08,215
A passing run can't show the option at
work, so let's see a wrong entry fail

113
00:07:08,215 --> 00:07:10,100
loudly, without spending anything.

114
00:07:10,600 --> 00:07:15,053
The run we'll use is the first one we
graded, before the skill had its rules, so

115
00:07:15,053 --> 00:07:16,500
it has plenty of failures.

116
00:07:17,000 --> 00:07:21,401
None of its graders is a script, and a
script grader is the one kind that can't

117
00:07:21,401 --> 00:07:22,125
be re-graded.

118
00:07:22,625 --> 00:07:25,325
We change into that run's folder.

119
00:07:28,167 --> 00:07:32,492
Then we re-grade it with today's eval
file, and with require pass.

120
00:07:39,200 --> 00:07:43,445
These are the failing blocks from that
run, each now with the budgets among its

121
00:07:43,445 --> 00:07:43,875
graders.

122
00:07:48,375 --> 00:07:52,720
Every budget passed in all eighteen
trials, three stimuli three times on two

123
00:07:52,720 --> 00:07:55,350
models; only the graders of the result
failed.

124
00:07:57,850 --> 00:08:00,400
So what's the exit code this time?

125
00:08:01,480 --> 00:08:04,705
It's one: a failing verdict is now an
error.

126
00:08:07,933 --> 00:08:12,471
Next, a trial that's over budget, which
none of ours is, so we make one for a

127
00:08:12,471 --> 00:08:12,883
moment.

128
00:08:13,383 --> 00:08:17,606
In the typo fix stimulus, we lower the
token budget from two hundred fifteen

129
00:08:17,606 --> 00:08:21,383
thousand to seventy thousand, which is
below what those trials used.

130
00:08:22,600 --> 00:08:26,400
We run the same re-grade again, and then
print the exit code.

131
00:08:35,593 --> 00:08:37,168
It's one again.

132
00:08:44,400 --> 00:08:48,650
That output is longer than one screen, so
here's the part that changed.

133
00:08:49,150 --> 00:08:54,283
These typo fix trials passed every grader
a moment ago; now they fail, on the budget

134
00:08:54,283 --> 00:08:54,650
alone.

135
00:08:55,150 --> 00:08:59,600
Seventy-four thousand tokens is over a
maximum of seventy thousand.

136
00:09:00,100 --> 00:09:03,250
The score still says ninety-eight point
eight percent.

137
00:09:03,750 --> 00:09:08,100
That's because going slightly over a
budget costs only a little of the score.

138
00:09:08,600 --> 00:09:13,279
With no scoring block, though, the score
decides nothing: every grader has to pass,

139
00:09:13,279 --> 00:09:14,125
and one didn't.

140
00:09:14,625 --> 00:09:19,275
So a trial that did the work and cost too
much is a failing verdict like any other.

141
00:09:19,775 --> 00:09:23,725
With that seen, the budget goes back to
two hundred fifteen thousand.

142
00:09:24,933 --> 00:09:29,697
Now everything, once: the full suite, with
the same options, into its own results

143
00:09:29,697 --> 00:09:30,108
folder.

144
00:09:30,608 --> 00:09:33,983
It spends thirty agent runs and six judge
calls.

145
00:09:34,483 --> 00:09:38,483
The table at the end has a row for each of
the four eval files.

146
00:09:41,613 --> 00:09:46,663
Several minutes later, every stimulus has
passed on both models, in all four files.

147
00:09:52,163 --> 00:09:54,213
Let's see what it reported.

148
00:09:55,340 --> 00:09:58,990
Zero again: nothing failed, budgets and
judge included.

149
00:10:02,200 --> 00:10:06,816
That was our second run of the full suite;
the first, a few minutes before it, ended

150
00:10:06,816 --> 00:10:07,475
differently.

151
00:10:07,975 --> 00:10:12,500
Here's the first run's table, with the
three stimuli Vally listed above it.

152
00:10:13,000 --> 00:10:15,400
They're two different kinds of trouble.

153
00:10:15,900 --> 00:10:19,975
On haiku, two trials ended in error,
because their sessions dropped.

154
00:10:20,475 --> 00:10:25,014
As before, an error isn't a verdict: the
trial didn't complete, so it says nothing

155
00:10:25,014 --> 00:10:25,900
about the skill.

156
00:10:26,400 --> 00:10:29,775
So we ran the suite again, and that's the
run we kept.

157
00:10:30,275 --> 00:10:34,730
The other is a real fail: on terra, a
question that shouldn't load the skill did

158
00:10:34,730 --> 00:10:35,175
load it.

159
00:10:35,675 --> 00:10:39,050
The loading grader that forbids that is
what caught it.

160
00:10:39,550 --> 00:10:44,275
Either problem alone makes the command
exit with one, and that first run did.

161
00:10:45,500 --> 00:10:48,975
One thing is left: the page that says what
we've agreed.

162
00:10:49,475 --> 00:10:54,650
Ours is a file named conventions, with ten
headings and a sentence or two under each.

163
00:10:55,150 --> 00:10:59,314
It opens with what earns a grader, code
before judge, where files are kept, and

164
00:10:59,314 --> 00:11:01,950
what we check before trusting a grader or
a judge.

165
00:11:02,450 --> 00:11:07,200
Then come what we run and when, how we
report a result, and what a run may cost.

166
00:11:07,700 --> 00:11:12,221
The last two are what to do when a run
goes wrong, and which models we used, on

167
00:11:12,221 --> 00:11:12,850
which date.

168
00:11:13,350 --> 00:11:17,812
That last one matters because models and
their prices change: a result means little

169
00:11:17,812 --> 00:11:20,500
without knowing which model produced it,
and when.

170
00:11:21,700 --> 00:11:23,900
So where does the skill stand?

171
00:11:24,400 --> 00:11:28,690
At the start, it was a first draft that
one model answered by making up a pull

172
00:11:28,690 --> 00:11:31,000
request number, behind a tick that hid it.

173
00:11:31,500 --> 00:11:36,000
With our first graders, one pair of
stimulus and model passed, out of six.

174
00:11:36,500 --> 00:11:40,805
Now the suites cover a conversation, a
loading test with near-miss requests, a

175
00:11:40,805 --> 00:11:45,000
judged quality that no pattern can see,
and budgets on every quick stimulus.

176
00:11:45,500 --> 00:11:49,350
In our last run, every stimulus in them
passed on both models.

177
00:11:49,850 --> 00:11:54,029
Every rule in the skill is there because a
trial showed it was needed, and every

178
00:11:54,029 --> 00:11:56,275
grader is there because a failure was
seen.

179
00:11:57,500 --> 00:11:59,775
Now, what does the routine cost?

180
00:12:00,275 --> 00:12:04,977
In our run, the quick suite's ten trials
cost about twenty-four AIU, which is about

181
00:12:04,977 --> 00:12:06,450
twenty-four cents an edit.

182
00:12:06,950 --> 00:12:12,137
The full suite's thirty trials cost about
sixty-seven AIU, with its six judge calls

183
00:12:12,137 --> 00:12:12,575
on top.

184
00:12:13,075 --> 00:12:17,433
None of the quick trials came near the
token budget: the largest used ninety-one

185
00:12:17,433 --> 00:12:20,375
thousand tokens, against two hundred
fifteen thousand.

186
00:12:21,600 --> 00:12:24,875
Those figures are for the two models we've
used throughout.

187
00:12:25,375 --> 00:12:29,806
Earlier, in the cost experiment, the newer
haiku model at low effort passed all

188
00:12:29,806 --> 00:12:34,125
fifteen of its trials on these same
stimuli, at about a fifth of an AIU each.

189
00:12:34,625 --> 00:12:39,425
On that one variant, a quick suite would
cost about one AIU an edit: a cent.

190
00:12:39,925 --> 00:12:44,244
Our conventions say how to make that move:
compare cost per passing trial, re-test

191
00:12:44,244 --> 00:12:47,825
the budgets on its saved trials, and run
the full suite on it first.

192
00:12:49,033 --> 00:12:53,483
One caution goes with that last table:
it's one trial per stimulus.

193
00:12:53,983 --> 00:12:58,229
In our two runs of the full suite, the
same loading question failed on terra the

194
00:12:58,229 --> 00:13:00,033
first time, and passed the second.

195
00:13:00,533 --> 00:13:04,985
A single passing trial shows that a
stimulus can pass; it doesn't show that it

196
00:13:04,985 --> 00:13:06,183
will pass every time.

197
00:13:06,683 --> 00:13:09,833
The suites are how we find out when that
changes.

198
00:13:11,033 --> 00:13:15,308
Before you go on: what are the five
checks, from cheapest to most expensive?

199
00:13:18,308 --> 00:13:20,983
Lint, after editing an eval file.

200
00:13:21,483 --> 00:13:24,133
The oracle, after changing a grader.

201
00:13:24,633 --> 00:13:29,208
Re-grading a saved run, after changing a
pattern, a budget or a rubric.

202
00:13:29,708 --> 00:13:32,733
The quick suite, after every edit to the
skill.

203
00:13:33,233 --> 00:13:37,433
Finally, the full suite, before sharing,
and whenever a model changes.

204
00:13:38,633 --> 00:13:41,233
That completes the first part of the
course.

205
00:13:41,733 --> 00:13:46,169
You can now run a quick suite after every
edit and a full suite before sharing, with

206
00:13:46,169 --> 00:13:50,077
commands that fail loudly on a failing
verdict, an over-budget trial, or a

207
00:13:50,077 --> 00:13:50,658
regression.

208
00:13:51,158 --> 00:13:55,283
You also have a one-page conventions file
to hand to a teammate.

209
00:13:55,783 --> 00:14:00,188
In the second part, the team behind our
made-up tool has put the skill inside its

210
00:14:00,188 --> 00:14:01,058
release tooling.

211
00:14:01,558 --> 00:14:05,258
Does it still hold when an app, not a
person, starts the agent?

212
00:14:05,758 --> 00:14:07,608
That's the next lesson.

213
00:14:08,108 --> 00:14:12,514
The exercises on the lesson page are worth
doing first, and the workflow guide there

214
00:14:12,514 --> 00:14:16,133
shows how a service can run the quick
suite on every proposed change.
